                <pb facs="0550" n="480" sameAs="frus:frus1867p2/pg480"/>

                <head>Mr. Seward to Mr. Plumb</head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 23.]</seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Department of State,</hi>
                            <hi rendition="#italic">Washington,</hi>
                        </placeName>
                        <date calendar="gregorian" when="1867-11-20"><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >November</hi> 20, 1867</date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir:</hi> Your despatch of the 31st of October, No.
                    27, which is accompanied by a copy of your correspondence with Mr. Farine, has
                    been received. In view of the delicacy with which the good offices of this
                    government in behalf of foreigners in Mexico must be employed, I think it proper
                    that you should make known to Mr. Farine that your communications with him must
                    be conducted unofficially and informally, and that such information as it may be
                    desirable to give concerning your proceedings in behalf of French subjects for
                    the information of the French government will be communicated directly to this
                    department. The French government will be informed of the instruction here given
                    you.</p>
                <p>I am, sir, your obedient servant,</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>WILLIAM H. SEWARD.</signed>
                </closer>
                <postscript>
                    <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Edward L Plumb,</hi> Esq., <hi
                            rendition="#italic">&amp;c., &amp;c., &amp;c.</hi></p>
                </postscript>
            </div>
